{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC6 discharged: change 2685 is deployed, and the measurements are re-taken\n\nAC6 held this epic (and, via the parent-first claim cascade, every child) because the 4.45 GB MCP\nfigure predated the projection fix and could not be trusted. Both halves are now satisfied.\n\n### 2685 is deployed — proven, not assumed\n\n```\nGerrit 2685  \"Reduce discovery reads without the bodies\"   status MERGED\n             submitted 2026-09-05 22:29:25   rebar-ticket: 9ea3-7d07-ea55-4496\n             merged as 1bffbd586a25\n\nrunning MCP container:  rebar-mcp-8e14d667da28-8092      (autodeploy names it for its commit)\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor 1bffbd586a25 8e14d667da28  ->  TRUE\n```\n\nThe container in service is built from `8e14d667`, and 2685's commit is an ancestor of it. So the\nrunning MCP **contains** the projection fix. This matters because \"merged\" is not \"deployed\" on this\nhost — the nginx edge proved that earlier tonight, where a merged change sat inert because that\nsurface is detect-only under autodeploy. Here the container name carries its own commit, which is\nwhat makes the deployment checkable rather than inferred.\n\n### Re-taken measurements (2026-09-06 01:29 UTC, post-deploy)\n\n```\nfree -m:   total 7813   used 4796   free 564   buff/cache 2453   available 2615   (33.5%)\nuptime:    load average 4.69, 4.99, 5.08      <-- 2 vCPU, ~2.5x oversubscribed\ndf -h /:   60G total, 42G used, 19G free (69%)\n\ndocker stats --no-stream:\n  rebar-mcp-8e14d667da28-8092   2.872GiB / 7.631GiB   37.64%\n  compose-review-bot-1           628.6MiB / 7.631GiB    8.05%\n  compose-gerrit-1               639.1MiB / 7.631GiB    8.18%\n  compose-opcert-1                40.55MiB / 7.631GiB    0.52%\n```\n\n**The old figure was substantially wrong, exactly as AC6 suspected — and wrong in the helpful\ndirection.** MCP measures **2.87 GiB**, not 4.45 GB. AC6 was right to refuse to let the plan rest on\nit.\n\nTwo corrections to figures used elsewhere in this epic's discussion: host total is **7,813 MiB, not\n7,987**, and there is **no ~0.97 GB quiet MCP baseline** — it sits at 2.2-2.9 GiB.\n\n### What the corrected numbers change, and what they do not\n\nThey do **not** dissolve the problem. Sizing each service for measured steady state plus one\nconcurrent gate still does not fit:\n\n```\ngerrit 3072 + mcp 3655 + review-bot 1343 + opcert 256  =  8326 MiB   against a 7813 MiB host\n```\n\nOver by **513 MiB before any OS headroom**, with MCP's steady state alone at 38% of the box. That\narithmetic is now based on post-2685 measurements, so it is evidence rather than an artifact of the\nstale figure.\n\nThey also **confirm the concurrency bound**. Against 2,615 MiB available: 4 permitted plan-reviews\nneed ~2,856 MiB and exhaust memory; 2 need ~1,428 MiB, leaving 1,187 MiB — just above the 1,024 MiB\nadmission floor, so a third is refused by the floor anyway. The count and the floor are\ncomplementary rather than redundant.\n\n### One finding that changes how the limits must be applied\n\nA compose `mem_limit` on the `mcp:` service **would never have bound the running container.**\n`mcp_run_new()` at `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:654` is a bare `docker run -d` whose own comment says\ncompose never sees it. `docker ps` confirms it independently: MCP is named\n`rebar-mcp-<sha>-8092` by that function, while the other three are `compose-<service>-1`. The bound\nmust be `--memory` on that `docker run`, pinned to compose by the existing parity oracle\n`test_docker_run_matches_compose_mcp_service`. Note also that every container reports the **host\ntotal** (`7.631GiB`) as its limit today — empirical proof that none is currently bounded.\n\nRecording this here so the epic's own AC6 no longer blocks its children on a measurement that has\nnow been taken."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788660655356618001,"uuid":"13c3977e-c1f9-4fb9-a127-fdbe7856a5d7"}